    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What type of hose nozzle is best?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, a fireman-style or thumb-control nozzle is best, because neither needs a squeezed trigger to stay open. Fireman-style nozzles are robust and good for cleaning, while thumb-control nozzles like Dramm's One Touch are ideal for daily watering. A pistol grip is fine if you prefer a trigger and mostly do short jobs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are metal hose nozzles better than plastic?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually. Metal nozzles, mostly zinc alloy with a rubber covering, survive drops and being stepped on far better than plastic ones. Solid brass is the most durable but usually comes in simpler twist designs. Material matters less than sealing, though, and a well-made nozzle of either type that does not leak is the goal."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does my hose nozzle leak at the connection?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Almost always because of the rubber washer inside the nozzle inlet. It goes missing, flattens or hardens over time, and water sprays around the threads. Replace it with a new hose washer and tighten the nozzle by hand. Overtightening with pliers crushes the washer and makes the leak worse. Keep a few spare washers in the shed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a hose nozzle increase water pressure?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. A nozzle cannot add pressure or water. A narrow jet feels stronger because the same water exits through a smaller opening at higher speed, but total flow stays the same or drops. If your spray is weak, look at your tap, hose length, hose diameter and kinks before replacing the nozzle."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best hose nozzle for arthritis?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Look for a fireman-style lever you can push with your palm, or a thumb-control valve that stays open without squeezing. The Green Mount GSFNG98 and the Dramm One Touch Revolution are both designed with this in mind. A lighter nozzle helps too, so the budget Green Mount thumb nozzle is worth considering."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a hose nozzle to clean my deck or driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For rinsing, yes. A strong jet or high-flow fireman nozzle shifts loose dirt and rinses away deck cleaners and concrete cleaners well. It will not remove ground-in stains, mildew or weathered wood fibers the way a pressure washer does. Use the nozzle to rinse and a cleaner or pressure washer for the scrubbing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I stop my hose nozzle from freezing and cracking?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Take it off the hose before the first hard frost, open the valve fully to drain trapped water, and store it indoors. Drain the hose too. Most nozzle failures in spring are cracked bodies or split seals from water that froze inside the valve or dial over the winter months."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
