        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A hose nozzle is a cheap tool that most people replace every couple of years without much thought, usually because it started leaking or the trigger broke. The difference between a good one and a bad one is not the number of patterns printed on the dial. It is whether it seals, whether your hand is comfortable holding it open, and whether it has the right spray for the jobs you actually do.
          </p>

          <p>
            We looked at the main nozzle styles sold for home use: pistol grips, thumb-control nozzles, fireman-style nozzles and twist nozzles. For each style we focused on established products with large numbers of owner ratings, checked manufacturer specifications for materials, patterns and flow, and read the hands-on testing published by Bob Vila and Reviewed. Where we cite a test result, it is theirs. We have not run our own nozzle test.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leaks counted heavily. A nozzle that dribbles at the connection or between settings wastes water and soaks your sleeve, and it is the most common complaint in owner reviews. Grip and control counted next, because you hold a nozzle open far longer than you think while watering. Patterns mattered least. Most people use three settings: a gentle shower, a jet and something in between.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ratings are Amazon averages with counts rounded down. Prices change constantly, and every nozzle here is inexpensive compared with the hose it is attached to, so we have not let small price differences decide the rankings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nozzle-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Pistol, Dial, Fireman, Twist and Wand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pistol-grip nozzles are the familiar type: squeeze a trigger to open the valve, and use a clip to hold it open. Most modern ones have a dial or turret on the front with several spray patterns. They are versatile and cheap. The weakness is the trigger, which tires your hand on long sessions, and the dial seals, which are where most leaks start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thumb-control nozzles replace the trigger with a lever or slider under your thumb that opens the valve and sets the flow. Dramm's One Touch range is the best known. Once you get used to them, they are much easier to hold open, and you can adjust flow without changing your grip. They are our first choice for people who water by hand every day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fireman-style nozzles have a big lever on top that you push to open, and many adjust the spray by twisting the head. They are robust, easy to operate with a weak grip, and usually move more water. Twist nozzles are the simplest of all, a solid metal barrel you rotate to go from off, to mist, to jet, with nothing to break, and brass versions like Dramm's 12380 adjustable nozzle can last for decades.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
